WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.660
When the hurricane hit, I was at my farm.

2
00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.800
I always go to my . . . I have a farm in Folsom.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.400
I'm a cowboy [laughing] and I raise cattle.

4
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.440
So I came on in here Tuesday.

5
00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:21.240
I didn't know about the flood because we had no communication.

6
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.500
I didn't know . . . I knew that the hurricane passed and everything looked okay.

7
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.720
Except all my trees were down, my fences were down and my animals were out.

8
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:36.180
Except for that, everything was fine. [laughing] So we . . . I drove in.

9
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.549
I tried to go down Tulane Avenue and the flood was there.

10
00:00:40.549 --> 00:00:44.640
And I tried to go down Canal Street and it was all flooded.

11
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.580
I had to get out of my car and wade and walk and swim and everything.

12
00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:56.840
But it just dawned on me then that this was a disaster beyond my ability to do anything.

13
00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:07.780
Because, you know, we were going to go pick up bodies and bring them to the coroner's office or wherever but the whole place was flooded.

14
00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.540
In front of my office there was ten feet of water.

15
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.460
You needed boats to get around.

16
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.720
Had you called in your staff to come as well?

17
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.820
No. First of all, there's no phones.

18
00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:28.960
But second of all is that the staff, most of them went to the office to ride out the storm because it's a solid building.

19
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.640
They went out and they rode out the storm.

20
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.140
I mean, it was the perfect place.

21
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.800
So we had about ten people in the office.

22
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:42.140
Little did I know, we would be trapped there for five days.

23
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:51.840
So as far as preparation, I mean, I had preparations in my own mind, but nothing of the magnitude of what happened.

24
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.380
So I knew we couldn't help, because we were held up.

25
00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:56.680
We couldn't get out of the office.

26
00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:56.885
Right.

27
00:01:56.885 --> 00:02:05.040
You know, once I got there in a boat, I couldn't get out until Friday.

28
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.340
What responsibilities did you have at the DMORT [Disaster Mortuary Response Team] site?

29
00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:20.620
To determine who gets autopsied and . . . It turned out not to be a responsibility because I said, "Everybody gets autopsied."

30
00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.260
What was your reasoning?

31
00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.620
What were you thinking when you were making that decision?

32
00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:36.420
These are . . . A thousand of these people are my people, from New Orleans, Jefferson [Parish], St. Bernard and Plaquemines [Parishes].

33
00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.280
A few from St. Tammany [Parish], not many.

34
00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:45.060
So I said, "These are my people and I'm treating them the same way I treat them when I'm in my office.

35
00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.040
We find out exactly what caused them to die.

36
00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:54.240
And as long as people died at the hands of the good Lord, then I'm happy.

37
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.700
But if they died at somebody else's hands, we're not happy."

38
00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.000
And that's basically what it was,

39
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.020
said we're not just going to scuff them off because we don't like the living conditions or we don't like the working conditions, for sure.

40
00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:15.867
But we are going to treat them like they are my people, my friends.

41
00:03:15.867 --> 00:03:22.860
And I did have a lot of friends, lots of friends.

42
00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.520
I saw a couple of them, too.

43
00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.820
But I could . . . I knew their names and whatnot.

44
00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:28.840
I didn't want to go see them.

45
00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:35.980
Because these people had been laying in the water, most of them, for two weeks.

46
00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:45.180
And then in the hot houses, in the attics, on the roofs, you know, the deterioration was tremendous.

47
00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:53.980
But I did see one of my dearest friends, I didn't plan to see him, he just popped up [phone ringing] so that was hard.
